diff --git a/docs/PLUTO.md b/docs/PLUTO.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/PLUTO.md
@@ -0,0 +1,177 @@
+# ADALM-Pluto output
+
+## Windows portable build
+
+The **Build Pluto Windows** Actions artifact now includes `pluto-runtime/` with
+portable Python, NumPy, pyadi-iio, matching pylibiio bindings and native libiio DLLs.
+Extract the complete package and keep that directory beside `gui_sdr_gps_sim.exe`.
+Leave **Python executable** set to `auto`. The application resolves the runtime
+relative to its executable, so changing the working directory or moving the
+complete extracted folder does not require editing settings. No system Python
+installation or pip command is needed for this portable build.
+
+An explicitly configured Python path still overrides automatic selection.
+Source builds without the portable runtime fall back to the platform's Python.
+The pinned Python bindings target libiio 0.x; do not replace them independently
+with pylibiio 1.x. The packaging script tests imports from an unrelated directory
+before the artifact is uploaded. This test does not open a radio or transmit.
+
+Open **GPS Simulator → Settings → ADALM-Pluto**. Pluto is the only desktop
+transmit backend, in Dynamic, Static and Interactive modes. HackRF, its USB driver,
+VGA/amplifier controls and network output selectors have been removed. File/null
+sinks remain in the Rust API solely for offline capture and regression checks.
+
+Use GNSS simulation only in an authorised, contained test setup. Follow the
+project's shielded-enclosure/direct-cable guidance. No hardware was used to
+validate this implementation; uninterrupted RF output and receiver acquisition
+still need a bench test.
+
+## Dependencies and build
+
+The application remains Rust-only to build: there are **no new Cargo dependencies,
+native link requirements or Cargo feature flags**. Pluto transmission requires its bundled or manually installed runtime. Its Python helper is embedded in the executable, so there is no
+separate helper script to copy next to a release binary. The following manual
+setup applies to source builds and custom Python overrides; Windows portable
+artifacts include the runtime described above.
+
+Install Python 3.10 or newer, NumPy, Analog Devices' `pyadi-iio` and the native
+libiio library. `pyadi-iio` installs the Python `pylibiio` bindings, but pip does
+not install the native libiio DLL/shared library. Do not install the unrelated
+PyPI package named `iio`.
+
+```sh
+python -m venv .venv-pluto
+# Windows:
+.venv-pluto\Scripts\python.exe -m pip install -r requirements-pluto.txt
+# Linux/macOS:
+.venv-pluto/bin/python -m pip install -r requirements-pluto.txt
+```
+
+On Windows, use an Analog Devices libiio release matching your Python
+architecture and make its DLL directory available to that Python installation.
+On Linux, install your distribution's libiio runtime/utilities, or build the ADI
+release. On macOS, install libiio with your package manager or build it. Use a
+compatible native library and Python bindings; mixing libiio major versions can
+fail during import. A conda environment containing `pyadi-iio` is another option.
+
+Check dependencies using the **same Python executable** you enter in the GUI:
+
+```sh
+python -c "import numpy, iio, adi; print(adi.__version__)"
+```
+
+See the official [ADI installation guide](https://analogdevicesinc.github.io/pyadi-iio/guides/quick.html),
+[libiio releases](https://github.com/analogdevicesinc/libiio/releases), and
+[buffer documentation](https://analogdevicesinc.github.io/pyadi-iio/buffers/index.html).
+
+## Settings
+
+| Setting | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| Device URI | Explicit libiio device selection, default `ip:192.168.2.1`. A full URI such as `usb:1.2.5` is also accepted; use `iio_info -s` to find available contexts. Bare hostnames/IP addresses are prefixed with `ip:` for compatibility. |
+| Python executable | Executable name or full path into a virtual environment. No extra arguments or shell quoting. Defaults to `auto`, preferring the runtime beside the executable, then the platform Python. |
+| Pluto TX gain | Hardware gain from -89 to 0 dB, default -80 dB. Negative values mean attenuation. Negative values mean attenuation; there is no external amplifier switch. |
+| I/O timeout | 100–60000 ms, default 10000. Applies to libiio operations and the supervised helper. Startup allows an additional 20 seconds for imports and device configuration. Stop remains responsive while waiting for the helper. |
+| Sample rate | Shared RF setting, 1–20 MSPS. The same integer rate drives IQ generation and Pluto. Readback mismatch is an error. The upper bound is a configuration limit, not a throughput guarantee. |
+| Centre frequency | Shared RF setting. Pluto validates the stock AD9363 range (325–3800 MHz); firmware range extensions are not assumed. |
+| Baseband filter | Shared RF setting. If disabled, Pluto uses the sample rate as TX bandwidth; an explicit override must be 200 kHz–20 MHz. |
+
+Settings are locked while any simulation mode is running. Pluto settings are session-only. The existing 3 MSPS GPS default
+is a reasonable starting point. Constellation/sample-rate requirements and the
+existing engine's modelling limits still apply; selecting Pluto does not add
+new GNSS models or resampling.
+
+## Public Rust API
+
+Use `SdrOutput::Pluto { config: PlutoConfig { ... } }` with
+`Simulator::builder().output(...)`. Both types are exported by `gps_sim`.
+`PlutoConfig::default()` supplies the GUI defaults. Set `uri`, `python`,
+`gain_db` and `timeout_ms` as needed. The existing
+`SdrOutput::PlutoSdr { host, gain_db }` variant now works as a compatibility
+shorthand with the default Python executable and timeout. Gain is negative dB.
+
+Use builder methods `sample_rate_hz`, `center_frequency` and `baseband_filter`.
+The former `hackrf_*` builder methods and HackRF/UDP/TCP output variants are
+removed in this Pluto-only version. The GUI retains 3 MSPS as the GPS default.
+At 4 bytes per complex sample this is 12 MB/s, before protocol overhead. Higher
+rates raise USB/network and CPU demand; the 20 MSPS configuration ceiling is not
+a sustained-streaming promise. The stock 20 MHz RF bandwidth also cannot contain
+the full BeiDou B1C spectrum; wider constellation models need separate validation.
+
+The PPB field is now labelled **LO correction**: it lowers the requested LO by
+the specified positive offset. It does not calibrate the oscillator or sample
+clock. Measure clock accuracy on the bench; do not infer it from a receiver fix.
+
+## Native 16-bit signal path
+
+The generator accumulates floating-point channel contributions, including their
+fractional gains, and rounds once to signed 16-bit I/Q. It no longer generates
+8-bit samples or shifts 8-bit values into a 16-bit container. Carrier tables use
+4096 floating-point entries rather than 512 rounded integer entries. Fixed scaling
+reserves 20% full-scale headroom for 24 simultaneous unit-gain channels. It preserves
+relative satellite amplitudes without per-buffer AGC. Excessive custom fixed gains
+saturate at -32768/32767 instead of wrapping, but clipping still distorts signals.
+The scaling is lower than the previous sc8 conversion, so previous gain settings
+do not imply the same measured RF output power.
+
+The wire/capture format is little-endian `I0:i16,Q0:i16,I1:i16,Q1:i16,...`, four
+bytes per complex sample. pyadi-iio receives complex64 values containing the exact
+int16 components; every int16 value is exactly representable, with no extra scaling.
+The **physical DAC remains 12-bit**, as specified by
+[Analog Devices](https://wiki.analog.com/university/tools/pluto/devs/specs).
+Native sc16 reduces software quantization loss; it does not create a 16-bit DAC
+or by itself guarantee better positioning.
+
+## Implementation and errors
+
+Rust launches an embedded Python helper with an argument list (no shell) and
+sends 262144-byte sc16 blocks: 65536 complex samples per block. Four generation
+buffers bound memory and latency; the libiio TX kernel queue is explicitly set to
+four buffers to match the drain timeout. At 3 MSPS each block covers about 21.85 ms.
+Channel 0 uses **non-cyclic** streaming: each push has fresh samples, so moving
+routes do not replay a stale waveform. No receive buffers are allocated.
+
+The helper acknowledges startup and each successful `tx()` call. Hardware
+back-pressure paces generation; no additional clock gate is added. Only one
+block is in flight through the supervisor, and the existing FIFO bounds queued
+generation. At natural EOF the helper allows four buffer durations to drain
+before destroying its TX buffer. Final IQ data includes the engine's existing
+zero-padded last block. Static loop passes reopen the backend, as existing
+hardware outputs do; seamless RF across pass boundaries is not guaranteed.
+
+Rust captures a bounded stderr tail for GUI errors, supervises stalled writes,
+and kills/reaps the helper on cancellation or timeout. The generator's FIFO is
+drained on TX failure so a blocked producer can stop. Python errors clean up
+the TX buffer in `finally`; forced termination relies on libiio/process resource
+cleanup. Missing Python/packages/DLLs, invalid settings, device-open errors and
+transfer failures do not silently fall back to another output.
+
+If imports fail, check the selected Python and native libiio installation. If
+opening the device fails, verify the URI and USB/network driver. If transfer
+timeouts occur, check host/USB performance and configured rate. No automatic
+USB discovery or driver installation is performed by the application.
+
+## Hardware-free checks
+
+```sh
+python -m pip install numpy
+python -m unittest discover -s tests -p test_pluto_bridge.py -v
+cargo fmt --all -- --check
+cargo check --workspace --all-targets --locked
+cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings -W clippy::all
+cargo test --workspace --all-targets --all-features
+cargo test --workspace --doc
+```
+
+Python tests use a fake radio to verify all 65536 signed 16-bit sample values, I/Q order,
+partial pipe reads, multiple fresh buffers, radio settings, non-cyclic mode,
+dependency errors, sample-rate mismatch and cleanup after transfer failure.
+They never open a real Pluto. Rust tests check quantizer headroom/saturation, configuration, URI normalization,
+FIFO ordering and the generated GPS spectrum/amplitude. Legacy GNU Radio scripts
+under `gnuradio/` are upstream sc8 tools and are not compatible with new sc16
+captures; use the Rust signal-chain tests or an sc16-aware reader. The Python helper tests also run in CI on Windows, Linux and macOS.
+
+Before relying on this backend, validate on physical hardware in the contained
+test setup: connection for each supported URI type, measured RF rate/frequency,
+amplitude, sustained transfer, natural completion, Stop, disconnects and static
+loop transitions. Software tests do not prove RF continuity or a receiver fix.
